1) How to build lcsoft
$ cd ~/sw
$ tar -zxvf ~/sandbox/lcinstall/lcsoft.tar.gz
$ cd lcsoft
$ bash
$ vi bin/setup.sh
....
   adjust SWDIR to suit your env
   check JAVA_HOME
....
$ source bin/setup.sh
$ sh bin/lcbuild.sh 2>&1 | tee make.log
.....
	if trapped into rootcint, type
	.q
.....
$ exit
C All needed libraries are ready now

2) Prepare your private physsim workspace
C This is the place where you are going to run your physsim generator
$ mkdir -p ~/work/physics
$ cd ~/work/physics
$ cp -p ~/sandbox/lcinstall/setup-physsim.sh .
$ vi setup-physsim.sh
.....
	adjust some env vars
.....
$ bash
$ source setup-physsim.sh
$ cp -a ~/sw/lcsoft/physsim .
$ cd physsim/pro/dbd
$ make 2>&1 | tee make.log


3) How to run dbd generators
$ cd ~/work/physics/physsim/pro/dbd

For instance, if you want to run tth generator, do the following:

$ pushd TTHStudy/prod
$ vi jsf.conf
....
	adjust job parameters such as Ecm, JSFHadronizer.Type, etc.
	most often only following ones need to be modified:
	<Roots> -- center of mass energy
	<PolElectron> -- electron beam polarization
	<PolPositron> -- positron beam polarization
	<LumiFileDirectory> -- beam spectrum produced by GuineaPig (Lumi_Linker files)
	<OutputFileName> -- output stdhep events file name
	<Ntries> -- number of events
....
$ jsf -b -q bases.C # to do bases integration; cross section is given in the end
$ jsf -b -q sim.C   # to do spring event generation;
This produces both hepevt and QuickSim data
C stdhep events are now produced; basically done if one would pass the generator events to detector simulation

Optionally you can try 
$ jsf -b -q gui.C   # analysis with QuickSim

$ popd

CAUTION
-------
This optional move might destroy the hepevt file data.
Make sure to backup the data.




